chapter_notes.1B:
Exposure assessment: this is a `limited_exposure_with_choices` case, not a nuclear-arsenal command case. Egypt had civilian nuclear authority, treaty diplomacy, and a live reactor project in 2022, but no public evidence of warhead command, brinkmanship, or operational nuclear use. I am not scoring.

**Reused evidence**
None. There was no accepted web evidence to reuse.

**New evidence**

- [President of the Republic](https://sis.gov.eg/en/egypt/system-of-government/executive-authority/president-of-the-republic/) establishes the office Sisi held in 2022 as head of state and head of executive power. This is basic authority context, not proof of any nuclear decision. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President of the Republic","publisher":"State Information Service","publication_date":"2025-09-24","url":"https://sis.gov.eg/en/egypt/system-of-government/executive-authority/president-of-the-republic/","claim":"The Egyptian president is the head of state and head of executive power under article 139 of the constitution.","locator":"article 139 excerpt","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official constitutional summary from the Egyptian state information service; the office definition is stable across 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Stable constitutional authority in force during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Defines Abdel Fattah el-Sisi’s office, but not any specific 2022 action.","contrary_evidence":["Constitutional authority alone does not show direct control over every security or nuclear decision."],"lenses":["1B.7","1B.8","1B.10"]}

- [IAEA Country List: Egypt](https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=EG) gives the treaty baseline for Egypt’s nuclear governance: NPT safeguards in force, but no CPPNM accession listed on this page. That supports a cautious institutional reading, with both positive safeguards and some gaps. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Country List: Egypt","publisher":"IAEA Office of Legal Affairs","publication_date":"2026-02-24","url":"https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=EG","claim":"Egypt has an NPT safeguards agreement in force with the IAEA, while the page lists several nuclear-security-related conventions as non-party for Egypt.","locator":"Safeguards Agreements table, lines 112-119; Agreements table, lines 34-42","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct IAEA legal-status listing with treaty registration data; good for formal status context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Treaty posture is stable context for 2022, though the page is current rather than a 2022 snapshot.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level treaty posture under Sisi’s government, not a ruler-personal act.","contrary_evidence":["This is a current legal-status page, not a 2022 contemporaneous record.","Non-party status on some conventions is a gap in security architecture, not proof of negligent conduct."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4","1B.7","1B.10"]}

- [Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons](https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?objid=080000028012079e) confirms Egypt ratified the NPT in 1981. That is important baseline context for 2022 disarmament diplomacy and safeguards. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"1981-10-30","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?objid=080000028012079e","claim":"Egypt ratified the NPT in 1981.","locator":"action details lines 14-23","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN treaty depositary record with direct ratification entry for Egypt.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Stable treaty status relevant to 2022.","ruler_attribution":"A state commitment inherited by and continuing under Sisi’s presidency.","contrary_evidence":["Ratification does not itself prove good-faith implementation in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

- [CTBT status](https://treaties.un.org/Pages/ViewDetails.aspx?chapter=26&clang=_en&mtdsg_no=XXVI-4&src=TREATY) shows Egypt was still only a signatory in 2022, not a ratifier. That is the cleanest formal negative on chapter 1B, though Egypt tied its position to broader regional imbalance. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty status page","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"2026-07-22","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/ViewDetails.aspx?chapter=26&clang=_en&mtdsg_no=XXVI-4&src=TREATY","claim":"Egypt is listed among Annex 2 states for the CTBT with a signature entry but no ratification entry on the status table.","locator":"status lines 87-105 and Egypt row in participant table","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN depositary status table is authoritative for treaty signature and ratification status.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Current status reflects the fact that Egypt had not ratified the CTBT in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"State-level posture during Sisi’s presidency; not evidence of a personal operational nuclear choice.","contrary_evidence":["Egypt publicly framed the non-ratification as linked to regional nonproliferation imbalance and universalization problems, not a desire to test nuclear weapons."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

- [Sisi hails Dabaa nuclear power plant as key cooperation project with Russia](https://sis.gov.eg/en/media-center/news/sisi-hails-dabaa-nuclear-power-plant-as-key-cooperation-project-with-russia/) is the strongest ruler-attributed 2022 source for his civilian nuclear posture. It shows him publicly sponsoring a peaceful nuclear-energy project, not a weapons program. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Sisi hails Dabaa nuclear power plant as key cooperation project with Russia","publisher":"State Information Service","publication_date":"2022-06-17","url":"https://sis.gov.eg/en/media-center/news/sisi-hails-dabaa-nuclear-power-plant-as-key-cooperation-project-with-russia/","claim":"Sisi publicly described the Dabaa nuclear power plant as an important cooperation project with Russia and part of Egypt's strategy for expansion into new and renewable energy projects.","locator":"article body on the president's virtual speech at SPIEF","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct Egyptian presidential messaging from 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler speech and sponsorship of the project.","contrary_evidence":["This is a development and energy frame, not evidence of nuclear-risk restraint.","It also deepens dependence on Russian cooperation."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.7","1B.10"]}

- [Construction permit issued for first Egyptian unit](https://nppa.gov.eg/News/Details?id=572) documents the regulatory approval before construction, including the safety prerequisite language and Rosatom training/fuel responsibilities. This matters for command-and-control and institutional risk management, but it remains a civilian infrastructure step. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Construction permit issued for first Egyptian unit","publisher":"Nuclear Power Plants Authority","publication_date":"2022-07-01","url":"https://nppa.gov.eg/News/Details?id=572","claim":"ENRRA issued a construction permit for El-Dabaa Unit 1 and the notice says the permit and excavation works were prerequisites for the start of the main stage of construction.","locator":"2022/07/01 notice body","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary regulator/owner notice with dated licensing details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Shows the state apparatus under Sisi advancing a civilian nuclear project through licensing, not personal command over weapons.","contrary_evidence":["The notice is self-promotional and does not independently verify long-term safety performance.","It evidences construction approval, not whether internal oversight was optimal."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.10"]}

- [Rosatom gets green light from Egypt to build Dabaa nuclear plant](https://www.marketscreener.com/news/latest/Rosatom-gets-green-light-from-Egypt-to-build-Dabaa-nuclear-plant-40858513/) adds an independent Reuters line: the Egyptian regulator granted permission for Rosatom to start building the country’s first nuclear plant. This confirms the official permit story from outside the Egyptian government. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rosatom gets green light from Egypt to build Dabaa nuclear plant","publisher":"Reuters via MarketScreener","publication_date":"2022-06-29","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/latest/Rosatom-gets-green-light-from-Egypt-to-build-Dabaa-nuclear-plant-40858513/","claim":"Rosatom said it received permission from an Egyptian regulator to start building Egypt's first nuclear plant at Dabaa.","locator":"Reuters lede in the syndicated story","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting is strong independent confirmation, though this is a syndication page rather than Reuters-hosted original.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"State-level regulatory action under Sisi's government.","contrary_evidence":["This confirms permission to build, not safe operation or ruler-level prudence by itself."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.10"]}

- [Construction of Egypt's first nuclear power plant under way](https://www.world-nuclear-news.org/Articles/Construction-of-Egypts-first-nuclear-power-plant-u) gives the first-concrete milestone in July 2022 and shows the plant under ENRRA licensing. That is strong civilian nuclear-program evidence, not weapons evidence. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Construction of Egypt's first nuclear power plant under way","publisher":"World Nuclear News","publication_date":"2022-07-20","url":"https://www.world-nuclear-news.org/Articles/Construction-of-Egypts-first-nuclear-power-plant-u","claim":"The first safety-related concrete was poured for El Dabaa Unit 1, marking the official start of construction after ENRRA issued the unit 1 construction licence.","locator":"article lede and middle paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Industry publication with dated construction and licensing details; strong for project chronology, weaker for political attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Confirms a civilian nuclear project advanced during Sisi's presidency, not a weapons decision.","contrary_evidence":["The source is industry-focused and may emphasize project success.","It does not independently evaluate security governance or accident risk."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.10"]}

- [Egypt begins construction of first reactor at El-Dabaa nuclear plant](https://english.ahram.org.eg/NewsContent/3/16/471838/Business/Energy/Egypt-begins-construction-of-first-reactor-at-ElDa.aspx) is useful because it adds Egyptian press confirmation plus the explicit IAEA-safety framing and ENRRA permit mention. It is still a civilian power-program story, but it strengthens the institutional picture. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt begins construction of first reactor at El-Dabaa nuclear plant","publisher":"Ahram Online","publication_date":"2022-07-20","url":"https://english.ahram.org.eg/NewsContent/3/16/471838/Business/Energy/Egypt-begins-construction-of-first-reactor-at-ElDa.aspx","claim":"Egypt began construction of the first reactor at El-Dabaa, and the article says the reactors comply with IAEA safety measurements and that ENRRA permitted Rosatom to start construction.","locator":"article body paragraphs 55-82","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reputable Egyptian outlet with concrete project and safety details, but still secondary reporting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Reflects the state project under Sisi, but not a personal nuclear-safety decision by him.","contrary_evidence":["The article repeats official assurances on safety rather than independently auditing them.","It reports project launch, not operational safety outcomes."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.10"]}

- [Egypt's delegate in New York stresses importance of fulfilling obligations on nuclear disarmament](https://sis.gov.eg/en/media-center/news/egypts-delegate-in-new-york-stresses-importance-of-fulfilling-obligations-on-nuclear-disarmament/) shows Egypt’s 2022 diplomatic line: nuclear-weapon states should fulfill disarmament obligations, and the Middle East should become a nuclear-weapon-free zone. This is favorable rhetoric and diplomacy, but it is not operational restraint. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt's delegate in New York stresses importance of fulfilling obligations on nuclear disarmament","publisher":"State Information Service","publication_date":"2022-08-05","url":"https://sis.gov.eg/en/media-center/news/egypts-delegate-in-new-york-stresses-importance-of-fulfilling-obligations-on-nuclear-disarmament/","claim":"Egypt said nuclear-weapon states should fulfill their disarmament obligations and that measures to reduce nuclear risk should not substitute for nuclear disarmament, while reaffirming support for a Middle East nuclear-weapon-free zone.","locator":"article body and quoted mission statement","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct 2022 Egyptian diplomatic statement published by the state information service.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"State position during Sisi's presidency; not a personal operational control decision.","contrary_evidence":["This is diplomatic advocacy, not evidence that Sisi changed Egypt's own risk posture in a measurable way."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.4","1B.9"]}

- [False logic of deterrence elevates role of nuclear weapons in security doctrines](https://press.un.org/en/2022/gadis3684.doc.htm) captures Egypt’s official 2022 line at the UN: it condemned the increased salience of nuclear weapons, called disarmament a legal obligation, and rejected normalization of nuclear threats. This is one of the clearest favorable sources for 1B.2 and 1B.4. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"False logic of deterrence elevates role of nuclear weapons in security doctrines, First Committee hears as general debate continues","publisher":"UN Press","publication_date":"2022-10-06","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/gadis3684.doc.htm","claim":"Egypt said nuclear disarmament is a legal obligation and condemned all nuclear threats, explicit or implicit.","locator":"First Committee meeting record, Egypt intervention","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN meeting coverage gives a contemporaneous record of Egypt's official statement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"State diplomacy under Sisi, not a private or military command action.","contrary_evidence":["Rhetorical opposition to threats does not prove equally strong action on safeguards or treaty ratification."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.4","1B.8"]}

- [Non-Proliferation Treaty Review Conference Ends without Adopting Substantive Outcome Document](https://press.un.org/en/2022/dc3850.doc.htm) gives a useful contrary-and-favorable mix: Egypt, speaking for the New Agenda Coalition, said the draft did not restore the balance between disarmament and nonproliferation and reiterated article VI obligations. This shows a consistent legalist posture, but also a continued insistence on the regional imbalance rationale. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Non-Proliferation Treaty Review Conference Ends without Adopting Substantive Outcome Document Due to Opposition by One Member State","publisher":"UN Press","publication_date":"2022-08-26","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/dc3850.doc.htm","claim":"Egypt, speaking for the New Agenda Coalition, said the draft outcome document failed to restore the balance of article VI obligations and reiterated the legal duty to pursue nuclear disarmament.","locator":"meeting summary paragraph on Egypt/New Agenda Coalition intervention","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN meeting coverage of Egypt's statement during the 2022 NPT review conference.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"State line under Sisi, not a direct presidential operational order.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is about global treaty politics, not concrete Egyptian implementation.","It does not address whether Egypt improved its own safeguards posture in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

- [Non-nuclear-armed states get nothing in return for fulfilling commitments except threat of potential annihilation](https://press.un.org/en/2022/gadis3690.doc.htm) is the best source for Egypt’s explicit CTBT rationale in 2022. Egypt said it had signed the treaty but had not continued because of imbalance in the regime and because some regional nuclear facilities remained outside comprehensive IAEA safeguards. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Non-Nuclear-Armed States Get Nothing in Return for Fulfilling Commitments Except Threat of Potential Annihilation, First Committee Told","publisher":"UN Press","publication_date":"2022-10-06","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/gadis3690.doc.htm","claim":"Egypt said it signed the CTBT but had not continued toward ratification because of ongoing imbalance in the nuclear regime, especially in the Middle East, and because some facilities remained outside comprehensive IAEA safeguards.","locator":"Egypt right-of-reply section in the meeting record","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN record of Egypt's own explanation, contemporaneous with 2022 treaty debate.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"State explanation under Sisi's presidency; useful for attribution but still a foreign-policy stance rather than a direct nuclear-risk act.","contrary_evidence":["This is self-justifying state rhetoric and may overstate the imbalance rationale.","It confirms non-ratification in 2022 rather than active disarmament implementation."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.6","1B.10"]}

- [Conference on the Establishment of a Middle East Zone Free of Nuclear Weapons and Other Weapons of Mass Destruction](https://www.un.org/en/unpdf/conference-on-establishment-of-middle-east-zone-free-of-nuclear-weapons-and-other-wmd) anchors the inherited regional baseline: Egypt was part of the long-running process and the 2022 third session was an active diplomatic track. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Conference on the Establishment of a Middle East Zone Free of Nuclear Weapons and Other Weapons of Mass Destruction","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-11-14","url":"https://www.un.org/en/unpdf/conference-on-establishment-of-middle-east-zone-free-of-nuclear-weapons-and-other-wmd","claim":"The UN lists the third session of the Middle East zone conference as scheduled for 14 to 18 November 2022, reflecting the ongoing regional process Egypt supported.","locator":"conference background and session list","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN background page for the conference process and its 2022 session schedule.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit for the diplomatic process.","ruler_attribution":"Regional posture under Sisi’s government, not an individual weapon-safety decision.","contrary_evidence":["A diplomatic process is not the same as successful implementation.","The page does not prove Egypt moved from advocacy to substantive regional breakthrough in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.8","1B.10"]}

- [Letter dated 12 April 1996 from the Permanent Representative of Egypt](https://www.un.org/unispal/document/auto-insert-186195/) is older, but it is the cleanest inherited-baseline source for Egypt’s long-running nuclear-free-zone posture. It matters because 2022 conduct is partly constrained by this longstanding diplomatic line. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Letter dated 12 April 1996 from the Permanent Representative of Egypt","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"1996-04-12","url":"https://www.un.org/unispal/document/auto-insert-186195/","claim":"Egypt transmitted the Cairo Declaration adopted on the occasion of the signature of the African Nuclear-Weapon-Free Zone Treaty and reiterated support for nuclear-weapon-free arrangements.","locator":"Cairo Declaration annex and transmittal letter","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN record of Egypt's own 1996 diplomatic position.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited baseline rather than 2022 direct conduct, but relevant to attribution.","ruler_attribution":"Historical state posture inherited by later Egyptian governments, including Sisi's.","contrary_evidence":["This is historical context, not 2022 evidence of Sisi's own conduct.","A longstanding norm does not itself prove present-day restraint."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**

I reviewed roughly 30 documents across Egyptian official pages, IAEA/UN treaty records, UN press coverage, Reuters syndication, World Nuclear News, Ahram Online, Egypt Today, Xinhua, CTBTO explainers, and regional disarmament pages.

I rejected or de-emphasized:
- Duplicate Dabaa coverage that repeated the same permit or first-concrete event without adding a materially distinct claim.
- Generic CTBTO FAQ/explainer pages when the UN treaty database already gave the cleaner treaty-status record.
- Broad 2023-2026 retrospectives that were useful for context but not necessary for a 2022 chapter dossier.
- Sources that were too generic, too off-topic, or not Egypt-specific enough to justify inclusion.
- One or two official Egyptian pages that were useful only as snippets because the page fetched with a 502 on open; I relied on the snippet only when it added a unique factual point.
- Old or historical disarmament records when a more direct 2022 source existed, except for the inherited-baseline Cairo Declaration, which is genuinely useful for attribution.

**Remaining gaps**

- Direct evidence of Sisi personally making any nuclear-safety, command-and-control, or crisis-escalation choice in 2022 is thin. Egypt had civilian nuclear governance, not a nuclear arsenal.
- I did not find strong 2022 evidence on whether Egypt had an Additional Protocol in force or on any strengthened safeguards beyond the basic NPT agreement.
- The public record is sparse on internal nuclear-security staffing, emergency planning, or cyber/physical protection decisions, so 1B.3 and 1B.7 remain only moderately evidenced.
- 1B.9 remains weak: I found general Egyptian references to cyber and outer-space risk in UN rhetoric, but not a ruler-specific, programmatic 2022 policy on dual-use catastrophic-risk governance.
- More search could add nuance on treaty diplomacy, but it is unlikely to change the core exposure classification: Egypt in 2022 was a limited-exposure civilian nuclear and nonproliferation case, not a weapon-command or near-use case.

chapter_notes.2B:
Exposure gate: limited. Your local priors show no Egyptian internationalized conflict events or fatalities in 2022, so the chapter turns on diplomacy, mediation, and regional restraint rather than battlefield conduct.

**Reused evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

**New evidence**
- Ukraine diplomacy and restraint. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President El-Sisi Speaks with Ukraine's President Zelensky","publisher":"Presidency of the Arab Republic of Egypt","publication_date":"2022-03-24","url":"https://www.presidency.eg/en/%D9%82%D8%B3%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1/%D8%A3%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%B1%D8%A6%D8%A7%D8%B3%D9%8A%D8%A9/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A6%D9%8A%D8%B3-%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%AF-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%AD-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%8A%D8%B3%D9%8A-%D9%8A%D8%AA%D9%84%D9%82%D9%89-%D8%A7%D8%AA%D8%B5%D8%A7%D9%84-%D8%A7-%D9%87%D8%A7%D8%AA%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A6%D9%8A%D8%B3-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%88%D9%83%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%8A-24-3-2022/","claim":"El-Sisi stressed the importance of dialogue and diplomatic solutions and affirmed Egypt's support for settling the Ukraine crisis politically through discussions and negotiations.","locator":"lines 124-129","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct 2022 presidential statement published by the Egyptian presidency.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler statement and public diplomacy; not evidence of operational military control.","contrary_evidence":["This is a diplomatic posture statement, not evidence that Egypt shaped the Ukraine war outcome."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.6","2B.10"]}
- Gaza ceasefire brokered through Cairo. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Israel, Palestinians agree truce from Sunday evening - sources","publisher":"Reuters via Euronews","publication_date":"2022-08-08","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/08/08/us-israel-palestinians-gaza-truce","claim":"Israel and Palestinian militants agreed to observe a Gaza truce as proposed by Cairo, with an Egyptian security source saying Israel had accepted the offer and a Palestinian source saying the ceasefire would take effect that evening.","locator":"opening paragraph / lede","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting is strong independent confirmation, though this is a syndicated Euronews page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Supports attribution to Egypt's mediation role under Sisi's leadership, but not a private command decision.","contrary_evidence":["The same escalation still caused civilian deaths and damage before the truce.","A temporary truce is not the same as a durable settlement."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.4","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- U.N. confirmation of Egypt's mediation role in Gaza. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ceasefire Between Israel, Palestinian Militants 'Fragile', Middle East Coordinator Tells Security Council, Urging All Parties to Uphold Truce, Safeguard Past Gains","publisher":"United Nations Press","publication_date":"2022-08-08","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/sc14994.doc.htm","claim":"The U.N. special coordinator said Egypt's efforts helped prevent the outbreak of a full-scale war in Gaza and allowed humanitarian relief into the Strip.","locator":"meeting coverage paragraph containing 'Together these efforts helped prevent the outbreak of a full-scale war'","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official U.N. meeting record with contemporaneous attribution of Egypt's role.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"External confirmation of Egypt's role; not a finding about internal Egyptian deliberations.","contrary_evidence":["The ceasefire was fragile and did not resolve the underlying conflict.","This confirms mediation, not long-term conflict resolution."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- Broader de-escalatory regional posture in mid-2022. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President El-Sisi's Speech at the Joint Press Conference with the German Chancellor","publisher":"Presidency of the Arab Republic of Egypt","publication_date":"2022-07-18","url":"https://www.presidency.eg/en/%D9%82%D8%B3%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1/%D8%A3%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%B1%D8%A6%D8%A7%D8%B3%D9%8A%D8%A9/%D9%83%D9%84%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A6%D9%8A%D8%B3-%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%AF-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D8%AA%D9%80%D9%80%D8%A7%D8%AD-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%8A%D8%B3%D9%8A-%D8%AE%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%A4%D8%AA%D9%85%D8%B1-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B5%D8%AD%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B4%D8%AA%D8%B1%D9%83-%D9%85%D8%B9-%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B4%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%AC%D9%85%D9%87%D9%88%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%A3%D9%84%D9%85%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%8A%D8%A7-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AA%D8%AD%D8%A7%D8%AF%D9%8A%D8%A9-18072022/","claim":"El-Sisi said Egypt would continue sincere efforts, in coordination with partners, to settle regional crises and address causes of tension, and that consultation should stay open to find practical solutions to the Ukraine shock and other challenges.","locator":"lines 133-139","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct 2022 presidential speech with explicit policy framing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler statement about Egypt's foreign-policy approach.","contrary_evidence":["This is broad diplomatic language, not a concrete settlement or ceasefire agreement by itself."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- Libya political process support. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President El-Sisi Meets President of Libyan Presidential Council","publisher":"Presidency of the Arab Republic of Egypt","publication_date":"2022-12-15","url":"https://www.presidency.eg/en/%D9%82%D8%B3%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1/%D8%A3%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%B1%D8%A6%D8%A7%D8%B3%D9%8A%D8%A9/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A6%D9%8A%D8%B3-%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%AF-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%AD-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%8A%D8%B3%D9%8A-%D9%8A%D9%84%D8%AA%D9%82%D9%8A-%D8%B1%D8%A6%D9%8A%D8%B3-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%AC%D9%84%D8%B3-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A6%D8%A7%D8%B3%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%84%D9%8A%D8%A8%D9%8A-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%89-%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%85%D8%B4-%D9%81%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%8A%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%85%D8%B1%D9%8A%D9%83%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%81%D8%B1%D9%8A%D9%82%D9%8A%D8%A9-15122022/","claim":"El-Sisi expressed firm support for the Libyan Presidential Council's efforts to create conditions for simultaneous presidential and parliamentary elections and to help end Libya's crisis through the will and choice of the Libyan people.","locator":"lines 122 and 166","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct 2022 presidential meeting note published by the Egyptian presidency.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler diplomacy toward Libya's political process.","contrary_evidence":["The meeting did not itself produce elections or a settlement.","Libya remained fragmented through 2022."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- Thaw with Turkey through back-channel diplomacy. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey, Egypt delegations held meets after leaders' handshake -sources","publisher":"Reuters via Euronews","publication_date":"2022-11-29","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/11/29/turkey-egypt-delegations","claim":"After the Erdogan-Sisi handshake, intelligence delegations met in Egypt and 'significant' discussions began on military, political and commercial issues, including energy projects.","locator":"lines 643-644","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting provides independent confirmation of the thaw and follow-on talks.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Shows Sisi as part of a de-escalatory bilateral thaw, though the reported meetings were not directly run by him.","contrary_evidence":["The source describes initial talks, not a completed normalization.","No substantive treaty or settlement is shown here."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.10"]}
- Regional summit with Israel and the UAE. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt, UAE, Israel hold summit talks in Red Sea resort","publisher":"Reuters via Euronews","publication_date":"2022-03-23","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/03/23/us-egypt-summit-israel-emirates","claim":"Sisi hosted a summit with the UAE and Israel, and Egypt said the talks covered energy markets, food security, and other regional and international issues.","locator":"lines 639-642","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting is strong independent evidence of the meeting and its agenda.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Sisi as host and participant in regional diplomacy.","contrary_evidence":["A summit is not by itself a peace agreement or a durable settlement.","The report is broad on content and does not show concrete conflict resolution."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.10"]}
- Iraq regional-stability framing. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President El-Sisi's Speech at the \"Baghdad Conference for Cooperation and Partnership\"","publisher":"Presidency of the Arab Republic of Egypt","publication_date":"2022-12-20","url":"https://www.presidency.eg/en/%D9%82%D8%B3%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1/%D8%A3%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%B1%D8%A6%D8%A7%D8%B3%D9%8A%D8%A9/%D9%83%D9%84%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A6%D9%8A%D8%B3-%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%AF-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%AD-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%8A%D8%B3%D9%8A-%D8%AE%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%84-%D8%A3%D8%B9%D9%85%D8%A7%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%84%D9%85%D8%A4%D8%AA%D9%85%D8%B1-%D8%A8%D8%BA%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%AF-%D9%84%D9%84%D8%AA%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%88%D9%86-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B4%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%83%D8%A9-20-12-2022/","claim":"El-Sisi reaffirmed support for security and stability in Iraq, rejected non-aggression and non-interference violations, and framed regional cooperation around sovereignty and good-neighborliness under the U.N. Charter.","locator":"lines 145-148 and 205-206","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct 2022 presidential speech with explicit references to non-aggression and sovereignty.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler statement expressing Egypt's security doctrine in regional affairs.","contrary_evidence":["This is a regional norms speech, not evidence of a specific conflict settlement outcome.","The speech is aspirational and broad."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- Diplomatic opposition to a contested maritime deal. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"مصر واليونان ترفضان اتفاقا مبدئيا بين تركيا وليبيا للتنقيب عن الطاقة","publisher":"Reuters via Euronews Arabic","publication_date":"2022-10-03","url":"https://arabic.euronews.com/2022/10/03/libya-turkey-ea3","claim":"Egypt and Greece rejected a preliminary Turkey-Libya energy exploration agreement in disputed eastern Mediterranean areas.","locator":"lede / opening paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting gives independent confirmation of Egypt's legal-diplomatic response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"State position under Sisi, expressed as diplomatic/legal opposition rather than force.","contrary_evidence":["The dispute remained unresolved.","Rejection of an agreement is not the same as a settlement."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across Egyptian presidential pages, Reuters/Euronews wire reports, U.N. records, and regional-diplomacy reporting. I kept the sources that most directly show ruler-attributed de-escalation, mediation, and lawful regional security behavior.

I rejected or de-emphasized:
- Duplicate Gaza coverage that repeated the same ceasefire event without adding a materially distinct claim.
- Generic background on Libya, Sudan, Ethiopia, or Gaza that did not show Sisi’s 2022 conduct.
- Later 2023-2025 Egyptian foreign-ministry pages that were useful for context but were out of period.
- Broad summit or speech material when it did not add a distinct peace/aggression claim.
- Articles about conflicts in neighboring states that described the conflict itself but not Egypt’s role in 2022.

**Remaining gaps**
- There is still no credible 2022 evidence of Egyptian initiation of war, proxy escalation, annexation, or other direct aggression. That is a real finding about the record, not a blank spot.
- The public evidence on proxy control, internal military accountability, and exact civilian-protection practice is thin because Egypt was not a belligerent in 2022.
- The strongest chapter evidence is diplomatic rather than operational. More search could add nuance on Libya, Sudan, or Gaza mediation, but it is unlikely to change the core exposure picture: Sisi in 2022 was a limited-exposure, mediation-heavy case rather than a war-maker.

chapter_notes.3B:
**Reused evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}

**New evidence**
1. The 2022 U.S. State Department country report, mirrored on ecoi.net, gives the broad baseline: unlawful killings, enforced disappearance by state security, torture, arbitrary detention, harsh prison conditions, protest restrictions, and weak accountability persisted in 2022. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Egypt","publisher":"USDOS via ecoi.net","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089216.html","claim":"The report says Egypt faced credible reports of unlawful killings, state-security enforced disappearances, torture, harsh prison conditions, arbitrary arrest and detention, serious restrictions on assembly and expression, and weak accountability for security-force abuses.","locator":"Executive summary lines 44-45 and section 1 lines 49-50, 68-75, 95-100","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative U.S. government reporting with detailed 2022 coverage, mirrored by ecoi.net.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers calendar year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Applies to Sisi-era state institutions and security services; not every incident is proven to have been personally ordered by him.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes some pardoned or released detainees, but says abuse remained pervasive.","Country-report format aggregates many incidents rather than proving command chains."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}

2. The same State Department report also documents a narrow remedial track: emergency-style powers remained after the state of emergency ended, and Sisi reactivated the Presidential Pardon Commission in April 2022, leading to some releases. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Egypt","publisher":"USDOS via ecoi.net","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089216.html","claim":"The report says Sisi ended the formal state of emergency in 2021 but kept similar powers in place, and in April 2022 he reactivated the Presidential Pardon Commission, which led to releases of political prisoners through year-end.","locator":"Executive summary lines 42-43","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Same authoritative country report, with a specific 2022 institutional-change claim.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to 2022 legal and detention practice.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Sisi as president, though the report does not show how far he personally directed each release.","contrary_evidence":["Reactivation of a pardon mechanism did not end arbitrary detention or abuse.","Retaining similar emergency powers cuts against a strong-restraint reading."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.10"]}

3. HRW’s 2023 Egypt chapter, covering 2022, says police and National Security agents continued forced disappearances and torture, and that authorities failed to investigate Ayman Hadhoud’s suspicious death in custody. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Egypt","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/egypt","claim":"HRW says police and National Security agents continued to forcibly disappear opponents, that detainees were subjected to torture and forced confessions, and that authorities failed to conduct an independent, effective, transparent investigation into economist Ayman Hadhoud’s death in custody.","locator":"lines 75-78","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Major rights organization with detailed, contemporaneous 2022 incident reporting and named cases.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers 2022 conduct and immediate aftermath.","ruler_attribution":"Security-force conduct under Sisi’s government; the report does not prove a direct presidential order in each case, but it is plainly attributable to the national state.","contrary_evidence":["HRW is advocacy-oriented and retrospective, so it should be weighed with other source types.","The report includes isolated release and dialogue references that do not offset the abuse pattern."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.10"]}

4. HRW’s same 2023 chapter says North Sinai in 2022 saw extrajudicial executions by army-affiliated militias and the army itself, plus a long demolitions campaign and uncompensated displacement. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Egypt","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/egypt","claim":"HRW says videos from July and August 2022 showed three extrajudicial executions of shackled or wounded men in North Sinai, with responsibility extending to army-affiliated militias and the army itself, while the army’s demolitions campaign left hundreds of families uncompensated.","locator":"lines 81-84","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Named, technically reviewed incident analysis from a leading rights monitor.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"This is the strongest direct domestic-safety evidence tied to the Sisi-era security environment in North Sinai.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes some family returns in late 2021 and early 2022, but the overall pattern remains coercive.","The army’s counterinsurgency framing is a claimed security justification, not a rebuttal to the abuse findings."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.6","3B.9","3B.10"]}

5. HRW’s same chapter also says refugees and asylum seekers in 2022 were arbitrarily detained, beaten, and forcibly returned, while women, black Africans, and LGBT people faced severe protection failures and digital targeting. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Egypt","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/egypt","claim":"HRW says Egyptian authorities and security forces subjected refugees and asylum seekers to arbitrary detention, physical abuse, and refoulement in 2022, and that women, black Africans, and LGBT people faced serious protection gaps and abusive policing.","locator":"lines 115-124","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed rights-monitor reporting with multiple vulnerable-group examples.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022.","ruler_attribution":"State protection failures and security-force abuse under Sisi’s government; some harms are societal, but the report assigns significant state responsibility for protection and policing failures.","contrary_evidence":["Some examples are social harms rather than direct state violence, so attribution should stay proportional.","The report mixes several vulnerable-group issues, so the chapter should not over-extend the claim beyond documented protection failures."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.8","3B.10"]}

6. Amnesty’s Arabic COP27 brief says Egyptian authorities arrested hundreds of people over protest calls, at least 151 were under prosecution, 1540 were investigated, release orders were ignored in many cases, and protest rules in Sharm el-Sheikh required 36-hour notice and limited the protest zone. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"مصر: الاعتقالات بسبب دعوات التظاهر خلال مؤتمر المناخ تكشف واقع الأزمة الحقوقية","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-11-06","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/ar/latest/news/2022/11/egypt-arrests-over-calls-for-protests-during-cop27-expose-reality-of-human-rights-crisis/","claim":"Amnesty says Egyptian authorities arrested hundreds over protest calls, at least 151 people were under investigation, 1540 had been investigated for expression or association, many release orders were ignored, and COP27 protest rules required 36 hours' notice and restricted protests to a monitored area.","locator":"lines 62-86","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights-monitoring with specific counts, named practices, and direct Arabic-language reporting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Sisi-era protest policing and deterrence around a flagship international event.","contrary_evidence":["Amnesty also notes some prisoners were released after the pardon committee was reactivated, so the picture is not pure one-way repression.","The article relies heavily on lawyers and civil-society reporting, though it cross-checks with documents and interviews."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}

7. HRW’s Arabic COP27 response says Egypt arrested hundreds nationwide after protest calls, including journalists, lawyers, opposition figures, and activists. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"مصر: اعتقالات وقمع إثر دعوات للاحتجاج","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-11-16","url":"https://www.hrw.org/ar/news/2022/11/16/egypt-detentions-repression-follow-protest-calls","claim":"HRW says Egyptian authorities detained hundreds across the country after calls for anti-government protests, including journalists, lawyers, opposition figures, and activists.","locator":"lede and opening paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent corroboration from a major rights organization, but still a short news release rather than a full investigation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the national security environment under Sisi, especially around COP27.","contrary_evidence":["Short press release with less detail than a full report.","It overlaps with Amnesty’s account, so it mainly corroborates rather than adds new factual depth."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9"]}

8. Reuters reporting, syndicated by Euronews, captured the wider COP27 climate of fear: activists said public demonstrations were effectively banned and access restrictions curbed civil-society participation. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt COP27 climate summit activists concerned voices will be curtailed","publisher":"Reuters via Euronews","publication_date":"2022-10-25","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/10/25/climate-un-cop27-access","claim":"Reuters reported that activists said public demonstrations were effectively banned in Egypt and that COP27 access restrictions would curb civil-society participation at the summit.","locator":"opening paragraphs and civil-society comments","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting is strong independent confirmation, though this is a syndication page rather than the Reuters-hosted original.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit before COP27.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects the protest climate under Sisi’s government, not a specific single order by him.","contrary_evidence":["The government said inclusion of civil society was a priority and pointed to additional NGO admissions.","This source is about access constraints and expectations, not the later arrest counts themselves."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9"]}

9. On 12 December 2022 the presidency said Sisi reviewed the first executive report on the National Human Rights Strategy, including reactivation of the pardon committee, the national dialogue, and the 2022 Year of Civil Society. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President El-Sisi Meets the Minister of Foreign Affairs","publisher":"Presidency of the Arab Republic of Egypt","publication_date":"2022-12-12","url":"https://www.presidency.eg/EN/%D9%82%D8%B3%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1/%D8%A3%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%B1%D8%A6%D8%A7%D8%B3%D9%8A%D8%A9/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A6%D9%8A%D8%B3-%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%AF-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%AD-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%8A%D8%B3%D9%8A-%D9%8A%D8%AC%D8%AA%D9%85%D8%B9-%D8%A8%D9%88%D8%B2%D9%8A%D8%B1-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AE%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%AC%D9%8A%D8%A9-12-12-2022/","claim":"The presidency said the foreign minister presented the first executive report on the National Human Rights Strategy, and that the report highlighted the reactivation of the Presidential Pardon Committee, the national dialogue, the Year of Civil Society, and other steps meant to expand public freedoms.","locator":"press statement body","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential-office release describing the government’s own reform narrative in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Sisi as head of the executive and the political figure behind these initiatives.","contrary_evidence":["This is self-reporting and does not establish effectiveness.","HRW and Amnesty both say the wider repression picture remained severe despite these initiatives."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.8","3B.10"]}

10. Ahram Online reported that 3,273 prisoners were released on Sinai Liberation Day after the Presidential Pardon Committee was reactivated, which is the clearest independent sign of limited remedial action in 2022. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt releases 3,273 prisoners granted presidential pardons on occasion of Sinai Liberation","publisher":"Ahram Online","publication_date":"2022-04-27","url":"https://english.ahram.org.eg/News/465369.aspx","claim":"Egypt released 3,273 inmates under presidential pardons after Sisi reactivated the Presidential Pardon Committee.","locator":"lede and opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reputable Egyptian outlet with a concrete outcome number, though it largely relays official action.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Sisi’s pardon decision and the committee’s operation.","contrary_evidence":["The release affected some inmates but did not end arbitrary detention, torture, or protest repression.","The report is about pardons and release counts, not due process reform at large."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across the 2022-2023 State Department report, HRW World Report 2023, Amnesty’s Arabic and English COP27 statements, Reuters/Euronews coverage of COP27 protest constraints, presidential statements on the human-rights strategy and pardon committee, Ahram Online’s release reporting, and a smaller set of UN and NGO references.

I rejected or de-emphasized:
- Duplicate COP27 pieces that repeated the same protest-arrest story without adding a materially distinct claim.
- Out-of-period material from 2023-2024 that was useful only as later confirmation.
- Broad economic or climate pages that did not establish a physical-safety nexus for this chapter.
- Purely generic human-rights rhetoric that did not add actual 2022 conduct.
- Sources blocked by technical errors when a mirror or a stronger alternate source existed.
- Older background pages when a 2022 or 2023 source gave the same point more directly.

**Remaining gaps**
- The public record still does not give a clean, independent chain of command for every abuse. The abuse pattern is clear; personal order is less clear.
- Exact counts for disappearances, torture incidents, and protest-related detentions vary by source and likely undercount actual incidence.
- The effectiveness of the pardon commission and human-rights strategy implementation remains uncertain. There were some releases and formal steps, but strong evidence of durable protection or independent remedy is absent.
- More search could refine the scale and timing of specific incidents, but it is unlikely to change the core chapter picture: 2022 Egypt under Sisi shows recurring severe repression, weak accountability, and only narrow, limited remedial action.

