**New evidence**
- [The 1st meeting of Egypt’s National Dialogue](https://sis.gov.eg/en/media-center/national-political-dialogue/the-1st-meeting-of-egypt-s-national-dialogue/) - State Information Service, 2022-07-05. The board of trustees’ first meeting marked the official start of the National Dialogue and was framed as open to public interaction through direct and electronic means. This is favorable but highly state-framed evidence. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The 1st meeting of Egypt's National Dialogue","publisher":"State Information Service","publication_date":"2022-07-05","url":"https://sis.gov.eg/en/media-center/national-political-dialogue/the-1st-meeting-of-egypt-s-national-dialogue/","claim":"The first meeting of the Board of Trustees marked the official start of Egypt's National Dialogue, with public interaction to be enabled through direct and electronic means.","locator":"opening paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct 2022 Egyptian state release about a ruler-initiated political process.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Sisi as president and initiator of the dialogue.","contrary_evidence":["Official framing does not prove genuine contestability or meaningful opposition access.","Independent monitors described the dialogue as heavily constrained."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.10"]}

- [Factbox: National political dialogue in Egypt follows years of crackdowns](https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/factboxnational-political-dialogue-in-egypt-follows-years-of-crackdowns-2857572) - Reuters via Investing.com, 2022-07-29. Reuters describes the dialogue as a test of whether authorities were prepared to ease a severe crackdown on dissent, with Sisi having steadily tightened his grip since 2013. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Factbox-National political dialogue in Egypt follows years of crackdowns","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-07-29","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/factboxnational-political-dialogue-in-egypt-follows-years-of-crackdowns-2857572","claim":"Reuters said the planned national political dialogue would test whether Egyptian authorities were prepared to ease a crackdown on dissent that critics called the most severe in recent history, and noted that Sisi had steadily tightened his grip since 2013.","locator":"lede and opening paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting is strong independent evidence, though this is a syndicated page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Sisi's regime trajectory and political control.","contrary_evidence":["The factbox is descriptive and not a legal finding.","It summarizes critics' views rather than adjudicating the dialogue's sincerity."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.10"]}

- [Egypt: CIHRS Position Paper on the National Dialogue](https://cihrs.org/egypt-cihrs-position-paper-on-the-national-dialogue/?lang=en) - Cairo Institute for Human Rights Studies, 2022-07-19. CIHRS argued the dialogue lacked fair-representation guarantees and that prior similar initiatives had been insincere, so it did not amount to genuine reform. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt: CIHRS Position Paper on the National Dialogue","publisher":"Cairo Institute for Human Rights Studies","publication_date":"2022-07-19","url":"https://cihrs.org/egypt-cihrs-position-paper-on-the-national-dialogue/?lang=en","claim":"CIHRS said the national dialogue lacked fair representation guarantees, the authorities had been unresponsive to opposition demands, and the initiative did not genuinely aim to start reform or address the human-rights crisis.","locator":"opening summary paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Credible Egyptian rights organization with specific critique of the 2022 process, though advocacy-oriented.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Sisi government because the initiative was announced by the president and run under state control.","contrary_evidence":["The paper concedes that some prisoner releases were a welcome step.","It is an advocacy position paper, not an external audit of dialogue outcomes."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.3","4B.10"]}

- [Egypt urged to unblock websites and free journalists as part of “national dialogue”](https://rsf.org/en/egypt-urged-unblock-websites-and-free-journalists-part-national-dialogue) - Reporters Without Borders, 2022-07-05. RSF said the government should unblock news sites and release imprisoned journalists as part of the national dialogue, and noted that 22 journalists were then detained. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt urged to unblock websites and free journalists as part of \"national dialogue\"","publisher":"Reporters Without Borders","publication_date":"2022-07-05","url":"https://rsf.org/en/egypt-urged-unblock-websites-and-free-journalists-part-national-dialogue","claim":"RSF called on the Egyptian government to unblock news websites and free imprisoned journalists as part of the national dialogue, noting that 22 journalists were detained at the time.","locator":"main body text after the opening paragraph","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent press-freedom organization with a concrete contemporaneous detention count.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Sisi's political opening, but the source focuses on media freedom rather than broader electoral competition.","contrary_evidence":["RSF is advocacy-oriented and does not prove every detention was personally ordered by Sisi.","The source also implicitly acknowledges some releases, so the picture is mixed rather than absolute."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.8","4B.10"]}

- [Violations overshadow reform pledges.. The annual report on the state of freedom of expression in Egypt 2022](https://afteegypt.org/en/research-en/2023/05/01/34214-afteegypt.html) - Association of Freedom of Thought and Expression, 2023-05-01. AFTE says authorities continued arrests, website blocking, and pressure on journalists and media in 2022, documenting 47 incidents and at least 63 violations against press and media. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Violations overshadow reform pledges.. The annual report on the state of freedom of expression in Egypt 2022","publisher":"Association of Freedom of Thought and Expression","publication_date":"2023-05-01","url":"https://afteegypt.org/en/research-en/2023/05/01/34214-afteegypt.html","claim":"AFTE said Egyptian authorities continued to restrict press and media in 2022, documenting 47 incidents and at least 63 violations, including arrests, blocking independent news websites, and judicial pressure on journalists.","locator":"introduction and 'Second: Freedom of the press and media' section","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed monitoring report built from AFTE casework and testimony, with concrete counts and categories.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Sisi government and its security/media apparatus.","contrary_evidence":["The report notes some releases through the pardon committee.","It is a monitoring report from a rights NGO, so it emphasizes abuse rather than state rationale."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.8","4B.9","4B.10"]}

- [No progress: the third quarterly report on the state of freedom of expression in Egypt (1 July - 30 September 2022)](https://afteegypt.org/en/research-en/2022/11/15/32744-afteegypt.html) - Association of Freedom of Thought and Expression, 2022-11-15. AFTE says the Egyptian authorities tracked and monitored social-media posts and increasingly targeted TikTok creators and anti-government content. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"No progress: the third quarterly report on the state of freedom of expression in Egypt (1 July - 30 September 2022)","publisher":"Association of Freedom of Thought and Expression","publication_date":"2022-11-15","url":"https://afteegypt.org/en/research-en/2022/11/15/32744-afteegypt.html","claim":"AFTE documented 14 incidents containing at least 28 digital-rights violations in Q3 2022 and said the authorities continued to track and monitor citizens' social-media posts, especially those critical of the government.","locator":"Section Three: Digital rights and key patterns of violations","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous monitoring report with explicit digital-rights documentation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Egyptian authorities under Sisi.","contrary_evidence":["AFTE documents patterns and cases, not a formal command memo.","The report covers one quarter, so it supports pattern evidence rather than a complete annual tally by itself."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}

- [Egyptian authorities arrest journalist Ahmed Fayez for reporting on Alaa Abdelfattah](https://cpj.org/2022/11/egyptian-authorities-arrest-journalist-ahmed-fayez-for-reporting-on-alaa-abdelfattah/) - Committee to Protect Journalists, 2022-11-14. CPJ reports the arrest of Ahmed Fayez by state security after a Facebook post about imprisoned journalist Alaa Abdelfattah, with charges including false news and terrorist-related allegations. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egyptian authorities arrest journalist Ahmed Fayez for reporting on Alaa Abdelfattah","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-11-14","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/11/egyptian-authorities-arrest-journalist-ahmed-fayez-for-reporting-on-alaa-abdelfattah/","claim":"CPJ said state security forces arrested journalist Ahmed Fayez on November 10, 2022, after a Facebook post about Alaa Abdelfattah, and charged him with spreading false news and terrorism-related offenses.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific named case from a leading press-freedom monitor, with detailed contemporaneous reporting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Sisi-era security practice against critical journalists.","contrary_evidence":["The account relies partly on local reporting and anonymous corroboration.","It is a single case, though it fits a broader pattern."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.8","4B.9"]}

- [Egyptian authorities detain 3 journalists since September](https://cpj.org/2022/11/egyptian-authorities-detain-3-journalists-since-september/) - Committee to Protect Journalists, 2022-11-09. CPJ said Egyptian authorities detained three journalists since September 2022 and called on them to cease detaining journalists for their work. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egyptian authorities detain 3 journalists since September","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-11-09","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/11/egyptian-authorities-detain-3-journalists-since-september/","claim":"CPJ said Egyptian authorities detained three journalists since September 2022 and urged them to stop detaining journalists for their work.","locator":"opening paragraphs and lede","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific contemporaneous press-freedom monitoring with named cases.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Sisi government's security apparatus.","contrary_evidence":["This is a short alert rather than a full country analysis.","It overlaps with other reporting on the same crackdown."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.8","4B.9"]}

- [Egypt releases 3,273 prisoners granted presidential pardons on occasion of Sinai Liberation](https://english.ahram.org.eg/News/465369.aspx) - Ahram Online, 2022-04-27. Ahram reported that Egypt released 3,273 inmates under presidential pardons after Sisi reactivated the Presidential Pardon Committee. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt releases 3,273 prisoners granted presidential pardons on occasion of Sinai Liberation","publisher":"Ahram Online","publication_date":"2022-04-27","url":"https://english.ahram.org.eg/News/465369.aspx","claim":"Egypt released 3,273 inmates under presidential pardons after Sisi reactivated the Presidential Pardon Committee.","locator":"opening paragraphs / lede","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reputable Egyptian outlet reporting a concrete state action and count.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Sisi's pardon authority.","contrary_evidence":["A large release does not negate the broader detention and censorship record.","The report is about an executive clemency event, not institutional reform."],"lenses":["4B.4","4B.10"]}

- [CPJ joins call for release of Egyptian journalist Alaa Abdelfattah as he escalates hunger strike](https://cpj.org/2022/11/cpj-joins-call-for-release-of-egyptian-journalist-alaa-abdelfattah-as-he-escalates-hunger-strike/) - Committee to Protect Journalists, 2022-11-03. CPJ said Abdelfattah had been imprisoned since 2019 and escalated a hunger strike timed to COP27, which underscores the continuing coercive environment for dissent and journalism. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"CPJ joins call for release of Egyptian journalist Alaa Abdelfattah as he escalates hunger strike","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-11-03","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/11/cpj-joins-call-for-release-of-egyptian-journalist-alaa-abdelfattah-as-he-escalates-hunger-strike/","claim":"CPJ said Alaa Abdelfattah had been imprisoned since 2019 and escalated his hunger strike ahead of COP27, with more than 60 civil-society organizations calling for his release.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Strong contemporaneous advocacy and incident reporting, though not an investigative finding.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to the Sisi government because the detention and prison conditions were state-controlled.","contrary_evidence":["This is a press-freedom campaign statement rather than a neutral audit.","It supports the broader repression pattern rather than proving a specific new 2022 action by Sisi."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.8","4B.10"]}

- [Presidency says foreign minister presented first executive report on National Human Rights Strategy](https://www.presidency.eg/EN/%D9%82%D8%B3%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1/%D8%A3%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%B1%D8%A6%D8%A7%D8%B3%D9%8A%D8%A9/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A6%D9%8A%D8%B3-%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%AF-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%AD-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%8A%D8%B3%D9%8A-%D9%8A%D8%AC%D8%AA%D9%85%D8%B9-%D8%A8%D9%88%D8%B2%D9%8A%D8%B1-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AE%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%AC%D9%8A%D8%A9-12-12-2022/) - Presidency of the Arab Republic of Egypt, 2022-12-12. The presidency said the first executive report on the National Human Rights Strategy highlighted the pardon committee, national dialogue, and Year of Civil Society. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President El-Sisi Meets the Minister of Foreign Affairs","publisher":"Presidency of the Arab Republic of Egypt","publication_date":"2022-12-12","url":"https://www.presidency.eg/EN/%D9%82%D8%B3%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1/%D8%A3%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%B1%D8%A6%D8%A7%D8%B3%D9%8A%D8%A9/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A6%D9%8A%D8%B3-%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%AF-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%AD-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%8A%D8%B3%D9%8A-%D9%8A%D8%AC%D8%AA%D9%85%D8%B9-%D8%A8%D9%88%D8%B2%D9%8A%D8%B1-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AE%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%AC%D9%8A%D8%A9-12-12-2022/","claim":"The presidency said the first executive report on the National Human Rights Strategy highlighted the reactivation of the Presidential Pardon Committee, the national dialogue, and the Year of Civil Society as reform steps.","locator":"press statement body","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential-office release describing the government's own reform narrative.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Sisi as head of the executive and political sponsor of the strategy.","contrary_evidence":["This is self-reporting and does not establish effectiveness.","Independent monitors said the broader repression picture remained severe."],"lenses":["4B.4","4B.8","4B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across presidential and state-information pages, Reuters wire reports, RSF, CPJ, AFTE, CIHRS, Ahram Online, and the U.S. State Department mirror. I kept the sources that best showed ruler-attributed political control, the national-dialogue reform narrative, press and digital repression, and limited remedial steps.

Removed or de-emphasized:
- Freedom House, V-Dem, RSF index pages, and similar structured sources where the relevant facts were already present in your local prior.
- Generic country summaries that repeated the same repression baseline without adding a 2022 ruler-specific claim.
- Older constitutional-amendment explainers and 2019 material when the 2022 dialogue, censorship, and detention record gave a cleaner fit.
- Duplicate COP27 protest stories that repeated the same arrest counts or access restrictions without adding a distinct fact.
- Broad NGO advocacy pages when a more specific alert, report, or official statement already covered the same event.
- Out-of-period 2023-2026 retrospectives unless they were needed to clarify the 2022 record, in which case they were only used as context.

**Remaining gaps**
- There was no 2022 election to observe, so 4B.1 and 4B.8 are inferred from the structural regime record and from the way the national dialogue was bounded, not from an actual electoral contest.
- The public record remains thin on de facto internal decision-making: who specifically ordered website blocks, journalist detentions, or digital surveillance is usually opaque.
- The most important positive evidence is limited and state-framed: the pardon committee, the national dialogue, and the human-rights strategy exist, but independent evidence of durable liberalization is weak.
- More search could sharpen the 2022 picture on party registration, court independence, and electoral administration, but it is unlikely to change the core pattern: Sisi in 2022 presided over a tightly controlled authoritarian system with selective remedial gestures and continued repression of dissent, media, and online space.

