- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Economy | Egypt, Arab Rep. | World Bank Human Capital","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://humancapital.worldbank.org/en/economy/EGY","claim":"World Bank data for 2022 show adult unemployment at 5.5 percent, adult inactivity at 50.1 percent, female inactivity at 82.5 percent, and undernourishment at 7.9 percent, indicating weak labor-market inclusion and household vulnerability.","locator":"benchmark indicators table","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank data portal with 2022 indicators, though presented as a dashboard rather than a narrative report.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"2022 indicator snapshot.","ruler_attribution":"These are household and labor-market outcomes under Sisi's tenure; they reflect broad conditions more than a single policy choice.","contrary_evidence":["The dashboard is descriptive, not causal.","Labor-market outcomes are shaped by long-run structure and external shocks as well as government policy."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary:
- I considered roughly 30 documents across IMF program statements, World Bank country updates, Reuters wire reports, CBE releases, the Egyptian presidency and State Information Service, Carnegie policy research, and later retrospective assessments from the U.S. State Department and the World Bank.
- I rejected redundant Reuters copies that repeated the same devaluation or inflation event without adding a distinct claim.
- I did not use generic World Bank or IMF overview pages when a date-specific Egypt update gave a cleaner 2022 fit.
- I excluded broad commentary that only rephrased the crisis narrative without specific policy, appointment, budget, or distribution evidence.
- I avoided overusing current dashboards unless they added a 2022 data point on household or labor-market conditions.
- I left out several post-2022 implementation reports and 2024-2026 reviews where they added little beyond confirming that the 2022 state-ownership policy remained a live reform track.

Remaining gaps:
- The biggest unresolved issue is implementation quality: there is clear evidence of announced reforms, but weaker evidence on how fully they were carried out inside 2022.
- Appointment evidence is thin beyond the CBE governor change; I did not find equally strong 2022 evidence on broader economic-team quality or delegation.
- Household distribution data are better than in many cases, but region-by-region and class-by-class impacts are still only partially visible.
- The military/state-enterprise footprint is clearly important, but exact 2022 profit extraction, privileges, and divestment progress remain opaque.
- More search could refine the separation between crisis response and intentional reform, but it is unlikely to change the broad reading: 2022 under Sisi was a crisis-management year with genuine stabilization moves, some pro-private-sector signaling, and major inflation, devaluation, and distributional stress.

