**Search and rejection summary**
I reviewed roughly 30 documents across IMF, World Bank, SIS/cabinet releases, Reuters syndications, Carnegie, AFTE, Amnesty, HRW, CPJ, and Ahram. I kept 12 distinct URLs because they provided the clearest ruler-specific effectiveness nexus. I rejected duplicate syndications of the same Reuters/AP stories, country-level governance indicators that did not by themselves show Sisi's execution choices, and broad macro or business-climate pages that were useful context but not evidence of ruler-attributed competence. I also deprioritized pages that repeated the same 2022 repression or economic story without adding a new implementation, adaptation, or coordination claim.

**Remaining gaps**
The biggest gap is direct evidence of the ruler's own internal program definition beyond the state-led, private-sector, and stabilization lines visible in official and IMF material. I also did not find strong documentary evidence showing whether Sisi personally overrode ineffective implementers in response to failed tactics, so 8B.8 remains partly inferential. Another gap is durable outcome attribution: several sources show policy launches and some implementation, but the evidence is thinner on whether 2022 meaningfully advanced the full portfolio beyond short-term crisis management. More search would help if you want a sharper distinction between direct presidential steering, bureaucratic follow-through, and outcomes driven by external shock or inherited state capacity.
